[Event "7) Fantasy Variation"]
[Site "Chessable.com"]

1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.f3 { This is the starting position for the 'Fantasy Variation'. The name sort of speaks for itself, and it's known for giving nightmares to the average lower rated player.

I consider this opening to be very interesting for White, but generally it is dangerous only in the hands of skilled players, since it requires quite a bit of move by move knowledge. From my experience with Black, players below 1800 tend to collapse pretty quickly if you play it properly. } 3...e5 $5 { Black has a variety of standard ways to get a very reasonable position, but they do require a decent amount of theory. Two perfectly playable alternatives are 3...dxe4 4.fxe4 e5 5.Nf3 Bg4 6.c3 Nd7 and 3...Qb6 4.Nc3 dxe4 5.fxe4 e5! . The main reason for sticking with the somewhat 'wild' 3...e5!? is that you can easily get a quick win, and it makes even higher rated players feel pretty uncomfortable. The line is a bit inferior compared to the other alternatives mentioned, but I think the risk-reward ratio is super profitable especially below 2000 online rating. } 4.exd5 { This is pretty common in lower rated games. } (4.c3 { This is another very common move for White which is not terrible, but the way most players handle it, Black can easily get a winning position! 4.c4? is really strange, but I've seen it many times below 1500. Just remember the key idea, which is ...Qh4+! 4...dxe4 5.fxe4 Qh4+-+ With a decisive advantage for Black already because 6.g3 Qxe4+ wins the h1-rook. 4.Be3 is somewhat common. I mean, the Fantasy players are super unpredictable, so I figured it's best to mention all the relatively popular moves. 4...Qb6! Immediately attacking the b2-square. 5.b3 Nf6 6.Qd2 exd4 7.Bxd4 Bc5⩱ With a small lead in development for Black and a pretty fragile situation for White. } 4...dxe4 5.fxe4 $4 { This is a very common mistake for White, played in over 5000 games. 5.dxe5 is a better move, but not threatening at all. 5...Qxd1+ 6.Kxd1 Nd7= With a fine endgame for Black. } 5...Qh4+ $17 { Black is already winning because 6.g3 Qxe4+ wins the h1-rook. It's important to remember that ...Qh4+ becomes a huge threat after 3...e5!? has been played. }) (4.Nc3 { This is the second most common move after 4. dxe5. } 4...dxe4 { Creating a threat of ...Qxd4! } 5.dxe5 Qxd1+ 6.Nxd1 Nd7 7.f4 f6 8.exf6 { 8.e6 may seem dangerous, but White is unable to hold on to this pawn: 8...Nc5 9.f5 g6! 10.g4 h5∓ White's position is collapsing because 11.h3 hxg4! wins for Black because the h1-rook remains unprotected. } 8...Ngxf6 $15 { We can continue with ...Nb6-Be6-Bc5 followed by ...O-O with a slightly better endgame. }) (4.dxe5 { This is by far the main move and what I expect you to face in about 2 out of 3 games. } 4...Bc5 $1 { Immediately targeting the vulnerable diagonal. With this variation we are trying to highlight the main drawback of the move 3.f3. Now, from my experience and according to the stats, below 2000 online rating, you are going to face both 5.Nc3 and 5.exd5 around 50/50. If you are planning to use this line for higher rated games, you should be expecting 5.Nc3 more often. } 5.exd5 { This is a clear sign that your opponent has no idea on how to play against our line! } (5.Nc3 { This is White's only move to fight for an advantage. The point is to meet 5...Qb6 with 6.Na4! and White is getting a small edge with precise play! 5.Bd3 leads to a comfortable endgame for Black after 5...dxe4 6.Bxe4 Qxd1+ 7.Kxd1 Be6 , preparing to play ...Nd7 without blocking the light-squared bishop. 8.Nd2 Nd7 9.f4 Nh6=∞ We are going to castle long followed by the ...f6 break (while keeping ...Ng4 ideas). 5.Ne2 Nd7 6.Bf4 In case of 6.exd5 the reply 6...Nxe5 gives Black a good position. 6...Qb6⩱ With a multitude of threats such as ...Bf2+ and ...Qxb2 with a small advantage for Black already. 5.Bf4 Qb6∓ Attacking both the g1-knight and the b2-pawn with a much better position for Black already.

This is White's only move to fight for an advantage. The point is to meet 5...Qb6 with 6.Na4! and White is getting a small edge with precise play! } 5...Ne7 $1 { I consider this to be the best practical try according to the stats. If you go for 5...Qb6 White is usually going to answer 6.Na4 , which can be somewhat difficult to deal with. After 5...Ne7 , it's pretty hard to predict what our opponent is going to play, so I will show all the decent options that White has. } 6.Bg5 { This and 6.exd5 are the two most common (from the relative small sample of games available). } (6.exd5 cxd5 7.Bg5 { This still gets played a lot. 7.Bb5+ Nbc6 8.Nge2 O-O=∞ With full compensation for the pawn because White is unable to castle short. We can continue with ...Be6 followed by the ...f6-break. } 7...O-O 8.Bxe7 Qxe7 9.Qxd5 $2 { This is a common mistake for online chess. Here we get to see Black's full potential when it comes to making use of White's king being stuck in the middle. 9.f4! This avoids immediate collapse. 9...Rd8 10.Nf3 Nc6 11.Bd3 Be3! 12.g3 d4 13.Ne4 f6⩱ Opening up the center so we can take advantage of White's king that is stuck in the middle thanks to the powerful e3-bishop. Black is already taking over the initiative. } (9.f4 $1 { This avoids an immediate collapse. } 9...Rd8 10.Nf3 Nc6 11.Bd3 Be3 12.g3 d4 13.Ne4 f6 $15 { Opening up the center so we can take advantage of White's king that is stuck in the middle thanks to the powerful e3-bishop. Black is already taking over the initiative. }) 9...Rd8 $1 10.Qe4 Rd4 11.Qe2 Nc6 $19 { We can continue with ...Bf5 followed by ...Nxe5 and ...Rad8 ideas while White's pieces are stuck. }) (6.Bd3 $1 { This is one of White's best moves. } 6...O-O 7.f4 $1 { Preparing to play Nf3. 7.Nge2 This move seems to be a bit more common online. 7...Ng6 8.f4 Nh4 9.Kf1 f6=∞ With full compensation for the pawn because White's king is quite vulnerable. } 7...Na6 $1 { Creating a positional threat of ...Nb4. I find this to be our best chance of getting reasonable compensation. } 8.Nf3 { 8.a3 Stopping any ...Nb4 ideas. 8...Bb6 9.Nf3 Nc5 10.Bd2 Bg4 11.h3 dxe4! 12.Bxe4 Bf5=∞ With reasonable compensation for the pawn thanks to our small lead in development. } 8...Nb4 9.a3 Nxd3+ 10.Qxd3 dxe4 11.Nxe4 Qxd3 12.cxd3 Bb6 $10 $13 { We can continue with moves such as ...Bg4-Rd8 followed by ...Nf5, with good compensation for the pawn thanks to the bishop pair combined with nice activity. }) (6.f4 $5 { This move seems to be super rare, but the computer quite likes it. } 6...Qb6 7.Nf3 Bf2+ 8.Ke2 { Creating a big threat of Na4 when the f2-bishop remains undefended. } 8...a5 $5 { This is important so we can meet 9.Na4 with 9...Qa7! } 9.Qd3 $1 { Preparing to play Be3. } 9...Na6 { Creating a pretty annoying threat of going ...Nc5! } 10.Be3 Bxe3 11.Qxe3 Nc5 $1 { The computer does not like 11...Qxb2 12.Rb1 Qxc2+ 13.Ke1 and Black does not have a way to follow up the attack. } 12.Rd1 dxe4 13.Nxe4 Qb5+ 14.Kf2 Nxe4+ 15.Qxe4 Qxb2 $10 { With a small edge for White with precise play according to the computer, but I feel like Black's position should be pretty safe after ...Qb6+ followed by ...O-O! }) (6.Na4 Qa5+ 7.c3 Bxg1 8.Rxg1 dxe4 9.f4 c5 $10 { Creating a big threat of ...Bd7 which is going to be very unpleasant for the enemy a4-knight. }) (6.Bf4 Ng6 7.Bg3 Qb6 8.Nge2 O-O 9.exd5 cxd5 10.Nxd5 Qxb2 11.Rb1 Qa3 { We can continue with ...Be6-Nc6 followed by ...Rad8-Rfe8 ideas. Important to notice that 12.Nc7 Qa5+! wins the knight. }) (6.Nge2 Qb6 { Creating a very annoying threat of ...Bf2+ followed by ...Qe3 CHECKMATE! } 7.Ng3 Bf2+ 8.Ke2 Bd4 9.Qd3 Bxe5 { Material is even, but we are slightly better because the enemy king is weak. }) 6...d4 $1 7.Na4 { 7.Nce2 Qb6 8.Nc1!? The computer comes up with this maneuver where taking the b2-pawn is risky. 8.Rb1? This natural move can easily get White in trouble. 8...Be6∓ Black is already better with threats such as ...Bxa2 and ...d3 ideas. 8...h6 9.Bxe7 Bxe7 10.Nd3 c5 11.b3 Qc7=∞ We can continue with ...O-O followed by ...Nd7-b5-c4 with full compensation thanks to the bishop pair and great activity on the queenside. } 7...Qa5+ 8.c3 dxc3 9.Nxc3 { 9.bxc3 Ba3! Creating a huge threat of ...b5! 10.Qb3 Qxe5= Black is able to regain the pawn with a completely fine position. } 9...h6 10.Bxe7 Bxe7 { Threatening to regain the pawn with ...Qxe5! } 11.f4 O-O 12.Nf3 Rd8 13.Qc1 b5 $10 $13 { With full compensation for the pawn thanks to the bishop pair combined with Black's very nice activity. A key idea is to meet 14.Be2 with 14...Qb6! keeping White's king stuck in the middle of the board. }) 5...Qb6 $1 { This move already puts White in a very awkward position. } 6.Ne2 $4 { You have NO IDEA how common this mistake is. I've seen even players around 1800 rapid online allowing this quick checkmate. } (6.Nh3 { This is a sad necessity. } 6...Bxh3 { Messing up White's kingside structure. } 7.gxh3 Bf2+ { Making sure that White is not going to castle this game. } 8.Ke2 cxd5 9.Qxd5 $2 (9.Nc3 Bg1 { Preparing to play ...Qf2+. } 10.Nxd5 (10.Qe1 Ne7 { Protecting the d5-pawn. } 11.Kd1 O-O 12.Bd3 Bf2 13.Qe2 Nbc6 { With full compensation for the pawn because White is struggling to coordinate his pieces. We can continue with ...Bd4 and break with ...f6 next! }) 10...Qf2+ 11.Kd3 Qd4+ { Picking up the e5-pawn. } 12.Ke2 Qxe5+ 13.Ne3 Bxe3 14.Bxe3 Nc6 $10 $13 { With full compensation for the pawn because White's king is stuck in the middle. We can finish development with ...Nf6 and ...O-O followed by ...Rfe8-Rad8 with tremendous pressure against White's king. }) 9...Nc6 10.Nc3 Bg1 $1 { Threatening to play ...Qf2+. } 11.Ne4 Rd8 $15 { With a small edge for Black because 12.Nd6+?? is a HUGE blunder because of 12...Rxd6 followed by ...Qf2+ (picking up the f1-bishop). }) 6...Bf2+ 7.Kd2 Qe3 { Subscribe to my YouTube channel if you ever get this checkmate! }) 4...Qh4+ { While Black has many ways of getting a reasonable position, I think going for the endgame is the easiest. } 5.g3 Qxd4 6.Qxd4 exd4 7.dxc6 { 7.Ne2 d3! This pawn sacrifice makes it difficult for White to finish development. 8.cxd3 Nf6 9.dxc6 Nxc6=∞ We can continue with ...Bf5-Bc5 followed by ...O-O (while constantly keeping an eye on ...Nb4 ideas). } 7...Nxc6 8.Bb5 Bf5 $15 { Black is already slightly better because we have annoying pressure on the c2-square. }
